- **`RefScope.IncludeRefPrefixes` narrows a request to one or more namespaces.** With any prefix set, a ref outside all of them is neither pushed nor pruned, in every kind — the filter is the single predicate the desired set, `TargetScope.Manages` and the prune set all ask, so push scope and prune scope cannot disagree. Exclusions still apply within an included prefix, and explicit `Mappings` bypass both as before. Every entry must start with `refs/`: a blank or `refs/`-less prefix scopes a request to nothing, which under a prune policy is a zero-plan success that repeats forever, so it is rejected at the request edge instead. git-sync's own `refs/gitsync/` scaffolding is the exception to the narrowing: a run manages only the bootstrap markers of branches it manages, minus any it excludes by name, so two syncs with different scopes sharing one target cannot delete each other's live resume state, and other scaffolding answers to the exclusions alone because prune is the only cleaner a stale marker has. Callers whose target refuses writes outside the included prefixes must exclude `refs/gitsync/` explicitly; the markers of branches a run manages stay prunable. The source ref listing is not narrowed: `ref-prefix` stays `refs/` under `AllRefs`, so an empty in-scope set stays distinguishable from a source that advertised nothing. ([#121](https://github.com/entireio/git-sync/pull/121))
